WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.610
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:10.490
About 350 episodes ago in this series, in
episode number 8, we talked about an amazing

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:16.890
paper in which researchers built virtual characters
with a bunch of muscles and joints, and through

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:22.240
the power of machine learning, taught them
to actuate them just the right way so that

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:29.140
they would learn to walk. Well, some of them
anyway. Later, we’ve seen much more advanced

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:36.210
variants where we could even teach them to
lift weights, jump really high, or even observe

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.660
how their movements would change after they
undergo surgery.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:45.730
This paper is a huge step forward in this
area, and if you look at the title, it says

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.900
that it proposes multiplicative composition
policies to control these characters. What

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:57.210
this means is that these complex actions are
broken down into a sum of elementary movements.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:02.770
Intuitively you can imagine something similar
when you see a child use small, simple lego

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:08.790
pieces to build a huge, breathtaking spaceship.
That sounds great, but what does this do for

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:09.790
us?

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:14.619
Well, the ability to properly combine these
lego pieces is where the learning part of

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:19.759
the technique shines, and you can see on the
right that these individual lego pieces are

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:25.780
as amusing as useless if they’re not combined
with others. To assemble efficient combinations

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:31.539
that are actually useful, the characters are
first required to learn to perform reference

00:01:31.539 --> 00:01:38.249
motions using combinations of these lego pieces.
Here, on the right, the blue bars show which

00:01:38.249 --> 00:01:42.570
of these these lego pieces are used and when
in the current movement pattern.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:47.680
Now, we’ve heard enough of these legos,
what is this whole compositional thing good

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.680
for?

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.009
Well, a key advantage of using these is that
they are simple enough so that they can be

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:59.289
transferred and reused for other types of
movement. As you see here, this footage demonstrates

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:05.950
how we can teach a biped, or even a T-Rex
to carry and stack boxes or how to dribble,

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:12.790
or, how to score a goal. Amusingly, according
to the paper, it seems that this T-Rex weighs

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:21.010
only 55 kilograms or 121 pounds. An adorable
baby T-Rex, if you will. As a result of this

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:26.530
transferability property, when we assemble
a new agent or wish to teach an already existing

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.819
character some new moves, we don’t have
to train them from scratch as they already

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:35.980
have access to these lego pieces. I love seeing
all these new papers in the intersection of

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.519
computer graphics and machine learning.

00:02:38.519 --> 00:02:42.740
This is a similar topic to what I am working
on as a full-time research scientist at the

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:47.750
Technical University of Vienna, and in these
projects, we train plenty of neural networks,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:53.010
which requires a lot of computational resources.
